{
  "_doc": "Curated GPU reference facts keyed by canonical chipset (canon_gpu_chipset spelling). Only facts that are INVARIANT for the chip name are listed: bus width, memory generation, reference board power and the chip designer's recommended PSU. Anything that differs between partner variants (VRAM capacity, OC power, cooler) is deliberately absent. Values come from the chip designer's published specification (NVIDIA / AMD / Intel product pages); where a chip name spans two memory configurations with different bus widths (RTX 3050, RTX 3060, GT 1030) the varying field is omitted. A derived cell only fills a null and is tagged derived:gpu.chipset_table. A row may carry '_requires': {field: [allowed values]} - it applies only when the product's own value for that field is known and listed (the chipset name 'Radeon RX 9070' also covers the 12GB/192-bit RX 9070 GRE). max_resolution is the display engine's digital maximum (7680x4320 on every HDMI 2.1 / DP 1.4a+ chip listed), a chip fact that board partners repeat; max_displays is deliberately absent: vendors print the card's output count (2-3 on a 9060 XT), not the chip's ceiling. tdp_w / recommended_psu_w are the chip designer's REFERENCE figures; partner cards often publish higher ones, and a sourced value always wins.",
  "_source": "Chip designers' published product specifications (NVIDIA GeForce, AMD Radeon, Intel Arc product pages), checked against vendor-published values already in the catalog (scripts/fill_report.py --check-tables).",
